.\" UNIX V7 source code: see /COPYRIGHT or www.tuhs.org for details.
.TH HYPOT 3M
.SH NAME
hypot \- euclidean distance
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <math.h>
.PP
.B double hypot(double x, double y);
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Hypot
returns
.PP
.IP "" 15n
sqrt(x*x + y*y),
.LP
taking precautions against unwarranted overflows.
.PP
.B Note:
v7 documented
.I cabs
here as well, taking a
.BR "struct { double x, y; }" .
It is not in this library.  The v7 source arrived wrapped in an
.B #if 0
and it went with it: there is no <complex.h> on this system \-
.B __STDC_NO_COMPLEX__
is defined \- and nothing declares
.IR cabs .
.SH SEE ALSO
exp(3) for
.IR sqrt ,
floor(3), intro(2)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
None.  The precautions are what make that true; see BESM-6 NOTES.
.SH BESM-6 NOTES
The care this routine takes is not a nicety here, because
.B an overflow is a fault and not an infinity:
past 2**63 the program dies.  The obvious spelling squares both arguments, and
x*x overflows for any |x| above 2**31.5 even when the answer is perfectly
representable.  Dividing by the larger argument first keeps the ratio in [0,1]
and the square in [1,2], so only the closing multiply can reach the top of the
range \- and it can only reach it when the answer itself is out of range.
.PP
The routine is otherwise v7's, unchanged.
.SH FILES
share/besm6/lib/libm.a
.br
lib/libm/hypot.c
